## 8.30 One last practical pattern — error introspection at the REPL

At the REPL, the most common workflow when something errors is:

```
> (some-verb 42)
error: type error — expected number, got string

> ,doc last-error
Return the most recent error raised in the current session.

> (define e (last-error))
> (error-kind e)
type-error
> (error-context e)
(:verb some-verb :expected number :got string :arg-index 0)
```

The context field told you exactly which argument was wrong and what shape was expected. That is often the entire debug — you now know your first argument to `some-verb` was a string when it should have been a number.

This is what \"errors as data\" enables. The message would have told you a similar thing but you would have had to parse the message. The context gives you the same information as pre-parsed structured data.

Reflex time. When an error surprises you, do not read the printed message twice. Bind and introspect.

Turn the page.

## 8.31 Where the kinds are catalogued — the error cards

Introspecting *one* error tells you about *this* failure. But the failures repeat, and Motoi keeps a catalogue of the KINDS. The error census (`stats/error-counts`, `stats/error-summary`) counts every error that escapes to the top level, filed under a normalized key — `unbound-symbol`, `not-a-procedure`, `class:TypeError`, `fuel-exhausted`. When a key recurs enough to be a pattern rather than a fluke, it earns a teaching card.

Those cards live in `scheme/reference/error-cards/` — one hand-authored card per kind of failure the census can produce. Each names what the wrong *means*, how to recognize it at a verb, how to detect it in code (with a runnable `guard` example), and the honest fix. It is the runtime companion to this chapter: here you learn to introspect *an* error as data; there you look up what a *kind* of error means and how others have fixed it. The honesty rule binds the shelf — every card's example is run through `./bin/motoi` and reproduces its output verbatim, so nothing aspirational ships.

Three of the cards name what the census *cannot* see, which is exactly the introspector's blind spot worth knowing: silent non-errors (a wrong value, nothing thrown), reader/expand-time errors (they fire before the eval boundary the census watches), and roster keys reserved but never actually thrown. Boot the shelf at `error-cards/README.slatl`; the machine index is `error-cards/MANIFEST.slat`.

